refcount: Reference counts and owners

Memory is reference counted (ADR-004). A *T is a gp::ptr<T>, which holds a reference count
and an address. new(T), &T{...} and a boxed variable make a block with a count of one;
copying a pointer increments the count, and the last copy to go out of scope frees the
block. Counts are atomic, so goroutines can share pointers.

A pointer into an object shares the owner of the whole object: &p.f has p's owner, &s[i] has
the owner of the slice's array, &x.f of a boxed x has x's box. So the object stays alive
while any pointer into it exists, and the pointer is never dangling. The annotation says
where a new owner is made and where one is shared.

A pointer parameter or receiver that the function only reads and writes through, compares,
or lends on to other borrowing functions is borrowed (ADR-086): the C++ parameter is a
gp::bptr<T>, a raw pointer without a count, and the call passes the caller's pointer as it
is (gp::lend(p)), or the address of a field (gp::borrow(&p->f)) instead of an aliasing
pointer. The caller makes sure that the object stays alive during the call: the pointer is a
temporary or a local that nothing can change, or the callee drops no pointer (it is pure);
otherwise the call copies the pointer first. A parameter that is stored, returned, captured,
converted to an interface or passed to a function that keeps it stays an owning gp::ptr<T>,
and the annotation says why. A parameter that the body never assigns (a string, slice, map,
interface, func or such a pointer) is a const T&: the call passes a temporary or a local
that nothing can change as it is, and copies anything else first.

Values that hold counts also copy them: a slice or a string copies its owner (one
increment), an interface shares its box, a gp::func shares its closure. A moved value
(static_cast<T&&>) transfers its count without touching it: the emitter moves where it can
prove that the source is not used again, as in s = append(s, x).

Cycles are not freed: use weak.Pointer[T] for the back pointer of a parent-child pair.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#pointers-and-boxing
See also: gocpp explain boxing, gocpp explain allocation
Also known as: arc, owner, owners, references, pointers

Example (refcount.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    type point struct{ x, y int }

    func main() {
    	p := &point{1, 2} // a new reference-counted box
    	px := &p.x        // shares the owner of *p
    	xs := []int{1, 2, 3}
    	pe := &xs[1] // shares the owner of the slice's array
    	*px, *pe = 10, 20
    	fmt.Println(*p, xs)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    struct point;

    struct point {
        int64_t x = 0;
        int64_t y = 0;
        bool operator==(const point&) const = default;
        std::size_t gp_hash() const {
            return gp::hash_fields(x, y);
        }
        static constexpr auto gp_fields = gp::fields(gp::field(&point::x, "x", false, false), gp::field(&point::y, "y", false, false));
    };
    static_assert(sizeof(point) == 16 && alignof(point) == 8);

    extern const gp::type_desc gp_td_point;
    extern const gp::type_desc* const gp_tdc_0;
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/refcount.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/refcount.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:7: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:8: p := &point{1, 2} // a new reference-counted box
    // gp[refcount] col 7: &T{...} allocates a new reference-counted box that holds the literal
    //     (gp::new_)
        gp::ptr<point> p = gp::new_<point>(point{1, 2});

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:9: px := &p.x        // shares the owner of *p
    // gp[refcount] col 8: &x.f or &s[i]: a pointer into an object shares the owner of the whole object
    //     (gp::ptr's aliasing constructor), so the object stays alive while the pointer does
    // gp[checks] col 11: p.f through a pointer: -> on a gp::ptr checks for nil and panics like Go
    //     (gocpp build -nochecks removes it)
        gp::ptr<int64_t> px = gp::ptr<int64_t>(p, &p->x);

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:10: xs := []int{1, 2, 3}
        gp::slice<int64_t> xs = gp::slice_of<int64_t>({1, 2, 3});

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:11: pe := &xs[1] // shares the owner of the slice's array
    // gp[refcount] col 8: &x.f or &s[i]: a pointer into an object shares the owner of the whole object
    //     (gp::ptr's aliasing constructor), so the object stays alive while the pointer does
        gp::ptr<int64_t> pe = gp::ptr<int64_t>(xs.gp_owner(), &xs[1]);

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:12: *px, *pe = 10, 20
    // gp[checks] col 2: *p: dereferences a gp::ptr, which checks for nil and panics like Go (gocpp
    //     build -nochecks removes it)
    // gp[checks] col 7: *p: dereferences a gp::ptr, which checks for nil and panics like Go (gocpp
    //     build -nochecks removes it)
        *px = 10; *pe = 20;

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:13: fmt.Println(*p, xs)
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
    // gp[checks] col 14: *p: dereferences a gp::ptr, which checks for nil and panics like Go (gocpp
    //     build -nochecks removes it)
        gp::fmt::Println(gp::fmt::as(&gp_td_point, *p), gp::fmt::as(gp_tdc_0, xs));

    // ---- ./refcount.gp:14: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg_desc.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg_desc.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    static const gp::type_desc* gp_full_point();
    const gp::type_desc gp_td_point = gp::named<point>("main.point", "main", gp::shape_lazy(gp::shape_struct(nullptr, 0), &gp_full_point));

    const gp::type_desc* const gp_tdc_0 = gp::td_slice<int64_t>(&gp::td_int);

    static const gp::type_desc* gp_full_point() {
        static const gp::field_desc gp_f[]{gp::field_desc("x", "main", &gp::td_int, nullptr, false, gp::offset_of(&point::x)), gp::field_desc("y", "main", &gp::td_int, nullptr, false, gp::offset_of(&point::y))};
        static const gp::type_desc gp_d = gp::completed(gp_td_point, gp::shape_struct(gp_f, 2));
        return &gp_d;
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
